  630: 	 *  G1-G5 pin who owns the register file and which CMMU is addressed -- but all of them
  631: 	 *  write SAPR.  After the split SAPR is in the PURGING arm, so the new store-only arm
  632: 	 *  had ZERO owner/cmmu coverage, and symmetrically every purge-asserting row leaves
  633: 	 *  cpu_nr and cmmu_nr at 0 because fresh() memsets dev.  A measure seat built SEVEN
  634: 	 *  mutants through that gap and FIVE were real defects, the smallest being one appended
  635: 	 *  clause:
  636: 	 *
  637: 	 *        if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && d->cmmu_nr == 0)
  638: 	 *
  639: 	 *  which is structurally the SAME attack as the historical `&& idata` mutant this file
  640: 	 *  already records as having survived sixteen rows.  Adding a second
  641: 	 *  `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` re-opened it on a selector instead of on data.
  642: 	 *
  643: 	 *  NOT THEORETICAL, measured on the rig: dev_luna88k.c:926-957 (add_cmmu_for_cpu) registers cmmu_nr 0 and
  644: 	 *  1 on EVERY boot including the single-CPU one, and the data CMMU (cmmu_nr 1, the one
  645: 	 *  memory_m88k.c:112 uses for every data access) takes 318,457 of the 613,342 SAR writes
  646: 	 *  -- 51.9%.  So `d->cmmu_nr == 0` silently discards half the SAR traffic, and a witness
  647: 	 *  driving SAR THROUGH THE HANDLER shows the consequence: the wrong page is flushed and a
  648: 	 *  stale supervisor translation is kept.
  649: 	 *
  650: 	 *  THE GENERAL LESSON, worth more than the rows: WHEN A ROUND SPLITS AN ARM, EVERY ROW
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/repro_m8sarpurge.c
    1: /*
    2:  *  TEST-FIRST REPRODUCTION for `m8sarpurge`.
    3:  *
    4:  *  #434 removed the purge from the READ side of the six-register arm, on the argument that a
    5:  *  read changes no translation so it owes no purge.  This is the same argument applied to the
    6:  *  WRITE side, where it holds for FOUR of the six registers -- and where 98% of the volume is.
    7:  *
    8:  *  WHO ACTUALLY CONSULTS THESE REGISTERS.  Enumerated over all of src/, not sampled:
    9:  *
   10:  *      SAPR   memory_m88k.c:135   read by m88k_translate_v2p (supervisor area pointer)
   11:  *      UAPR   memory_m88k.c:137   read by m88k_translate_v2p (user area pointer)
   12:  *      SAR    dev_m8820x.c:110    read ONCE, as the address operand of a flush COMMAND --
   13:  *                                 and that command's own arm already purges (dev_m8820x.c:187)
   14:  *      PFSR   memory_m88k.c:382,383, dev_luna88k.c:614   WRITTEN by the fault path; never read
   15:  *      PFAR   memory_m88k.c:399,405                      WRITTEN by the fault path; never read
   16:  *      SCTR   -- appears nowhere in src/ outside its own case label.  Never read at all.
   17:  *      [#556, 2026-10-02 -- a records correction; the text above is kept as it was written] SCTR has register and
   18:  *      bit definitions; a guest write stores it in the SCTR case, and a guest read returns it through the generic
   19:  *      register accessor before the switch. No translation consumer reads SCTR.  (hbundle_regress31:F11:
   20:  *      regress/artifacts/astraretro_harness_20260926/HARNESS_DISPOSITIONS.md)
   21:  *
   22:  *  So a guest write to SAPR or UAPR genuinely changes what the translator will do, and owes a
   23:  *  purge.  A guest write to SAR, PFSR, PFAR or SCTR cannot change any translation IN THIS MODEL,
   24:  *  and owes nothing -- by exactly #434's argument, pointed at the other half of the same arm.

