  330: 
  331: ====================================================================================================================
  332: 7. WHERE MY READING DIFFERS FROM THE BRIEF, THE RULINGS OR THE SPEC (each with its line; detail in BUILDER_QUESTIONS)
  333: ====================================================================================================================
  334:   R-a  C5 / CHECKS #4 / PASS1_RULINGS line 17-19 ("dev_m8820x.c:108 dereferences ... for the ACCESSING cpu") is NOT
  335:        what the code does.  :108 is in m8820x_command(struct cpu *cpu, ...) (:106), whose ONLY caller is :284,
  336:        `m8820x_command(c, d)', with `c = cpu->machine->cpus[d->cpu_nr]' (:250).  So :108 dereferences the CMMU
  337:        OWNER's slot, exactly as :251-252 do.  On mvme187 every CMMU window decodes cpu_nr 0 (:542-543, addr2 0/1),
  338:        and cpus[0] owns both CMMUs, so a CMMU access by CMMU-less cpu 1 reads/writes cpu 0's CMMUs and dereferences
  339:        nothing NULL.  C5 as filed is refuted by reading; the NULL dereference at :250-252 exists only in C4's
  340:        configuration (a random non-zero bootstrap cpu).  The B5c constraint "cpu 1 never touches the CMMU window"
  341:        stays harmless (this design keeps it), but its stated reason is wrong.
  342:   R-b  A6's ordering warrant (grok:32 "order matters because NBSG uses PDT_BITS and PG_BITS") is wrong: NBSG
  343:        (m8820x.h:182) is an object-like macro, expanded only at a use, and nothing in src/ uses it; upstream itself
  344:        placed the include BEFORE m8820x_pte.h (trunk_src.diff:1921-1922).  Either order compiles; "directly after
  345:        :53" is fine, the reason is not.
  346:   R-c  B5c's dispatcher premise ("both cpus start at one pc, so the program begins with a RAM-flag dispatcher") does
  347:        not follow: `focus 1' (debugger_cmds.c:1125) makes `pc=' / `rN=' / `VBR=' act on cpu 1 (debugger_expr.c:167
  348:        resolves names as cpu[debugger_cur_cpu].<name>; cpu_m88k.c:141-153 registers pc, r0-r31 and the control
  349:        registers by name), and `reg 1' / `reg 1,0' (debugger_cmds.c:1653) read cpu 1's registers.  Markers can live
  350:        in registers (the probe's existing path_of idiom), not RAM.
  351:   R-d  Stepping with two RUNNING cpus advances BOTH, one instruction each per step (machine_run,
  352:        machines/machine.c:710; single-step run_instr executes one instruction, cpu_dyntrans.c:272-327).  The design
  353:        therefore pauses cpu 0 before cpu 1 runs, so each cpu's step count is exact.
  354:   R-e  B5b's "already killed by `ok regs'" dissent (GLM-Q12:34) is correctly rejected by the ruling: under L7 the
  355:        accepted access is unaffected; only a NO_EXCEPTIONS access to a refusing device changes -- the dump.
  356:   R-f  The 24-row detector's grok:98-111 table (B6) predates the new rows; section 5 above re-derives the red set for
  357:        31 rows.  Notably L6 reds ifetch dcmmu too, and L18/L19's named channels are unreachable (section 4).
